+.. _cli_safe_import:
+
+Safe imports and invariants
+==========================
+
+Safe mode makes the entire import atomic, rather than committing each batch.
+Enable it for a database, then opt in on each write with ``--safe-mode``:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils enable-safe-import data.db
+    sqlite-utils add-import-invariant data.db items 'quantity >= 0'
+    sqlite-utils add-import-invariant data.db items 'SUM(quantity) <= 1000'
+    sqlite-utils insert data.db items items.json --pk id --safe-mode
+    sqlite-utils upsert data.db items changes.json --pk id --safe-mode
+    sqlite-utils insert data.db items items.csv --csv --alter --safe-mode
+    sqlite-utils bulk data.db 'UPDATE items SET quantity = :quantity WHERE id = :id' changes.json --safe-mode
+
+JSON is the default format. In safe mode, file suffixes ``.csv``, ``.tsv``,
+``.jsonl`` and ``.ndjson`` infer the format when no explicit format is selected.
+For stdin, or to override inference, use the existing ``--csv``, ``--tsv`` and
+``--nl`` options. ``--batch-size`` controls processing chunks
+in safe mode, not intermediate commits. CSV type detection and schema alterations
+are inside the checkpoint too. ``--strict`` still means SQLite STRICT tables on
+the CLI; it is not the Python safe-import exception policy.
+
+Each successful ``add-import-invariant PATH TABLE SQL`` prints an opaque ID.
+Invariants and the enable/disable setting persist when the database is reopened.
+Invariants may be registered before creating the table. A missing table or an
+invalid SQL expression is a validation failure, not a skipped check.
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils list-import-invariants data.db items
+    sqlite-utils validate-import-invariants data.db items
+    sqlite-utils remove-import-invariant data.db items INVARIANT_ID
+    sqlite-utils disable-safe-import data.db
+
+``list-import-invariants`` prints each ID followed by its SQL. Validation prints
+``PASS`` or ``FAIL`` and includes the IDs, expressions and errors of failing
+invariants. The validation command exits zero even for failing invariants; it is
+a diagnostic command. In contrast, safe ``insert``, ``upsert`` and ``bulk`` exit
+zero only when their checkpoint is successfully released. Disabled safe import,
+parse/SQL errors and invariant failures produce a non-zero exit and an error
+message. A failed operation rolls back its data and transactional schema changes,
+including columns, indexes and triggers; earlier batches are not left behind.
+
+All registered table invariants are checked after a safe write, including tables
+changed by triggers. A ``SELECT`` invariant uses the truthiness of its first
+column in its first row (no row or NULL fails). Other SQL is an expression:
+aggregates such as ``COUNT(*)``, ``SUM(quantity)`` and ``AVG(quantity)`` are
+evaluated once over the table, while row expressions must be true for every row.
+An empty table satisfies a row expression vacuously. Use ``COUNT(*) > 0`` to
+require a nonempty table. SQL errors are reported with the failing invariant ID.
+Only use trusted invariant SQL and trusted conversion code.
+
+Enabling safe import does not silently change ordinary writes: omit
+``--safe-mode`` to retain existing behavior. Disabling does not delete invariants.
+See :ref:`python_api_safe_import` for connection-local checkpoints and Python
+import helpers.
+
